      "text": "To solve the problem, we will use the tangent addition formula:\n\n\\[\n\\tan(a + b) = \\frac{\\tan a + \\tan b}{1 - \\tan a \\tan b}\n\\]\n\nIn our case, we need to find \\(\\tan\\left(x + \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\\), where \\(x = \\tan^{-1}(2)\\). So,\n\n\\[\n\\tan \\left(x + \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\right) = \\frac{\\tan x + \\tan \\left( \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\right)}{1 - \\tan x \\tan \\left( \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\right)}\n\\]\n\nWe know that \\(\\tan \\left( \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\right) = 1\\), so substituting this in, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\tan \\left(x + \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\right) = \\frac{\\tan x + 1}{1 - \\tan x}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\tan x = 2\\), we can substitute this in as well:\n\n\\[\n\\tan \\left(x + \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\right) = \\frac{2 + 1}{1 - 2} = \\frac{3}{-1} = -3\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is \\(-3\\). Let's verify this by coding the solution in Python.\n```python\nimport math\n\n# Given value\ntan_x = 2\n\n# Using the tangent addition formula\ntan_x_plus_pi_over_4 = (tan_x + 1) / (1 - tan_x)\nprint(tan_x_plus_pi_over_4)\n```\n```output\n-3.0\n```\nThe calculations confirm that \\(\\tan\\left(x + \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) = -3\\). Therefore, the final answer is \\(\\boxed{-3}\\).",
